Pancreatitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, Pain relief leaning forward, Profuse vomiting, Serum lipase >3x normal

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-049
Reason for Visit: Acute onset severe, unrelenting upper abdominal pain shooting into the back, with continuous vomiting preventing any fluid intake.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of severe, escalating abdominal pain and a total inability to tolerate oral intake, necessitating urgent clinical assessment to rule out a surgical abdomen, initiate parenteral antiemetic therapy, and assess the need for immediate hospital transfer prior to the next in-hours period to prevent profound dehydration and clinical deterioration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain is agonizing and goes straight through to my spine, and I cannot stop throwing up even though my stomach is empty."
History of Present Illness: Sudden onset of severe, continuous epigastric abdominal pain radiating directly through to the back commencing 6 hours ago. Patient notes partial pain relief characteristically achieved by sitting up and leaning forward. Accompanied by severe nausea and profuse, unrelenting vomiting. Denies recent significant alcohol intake; reports a prior history of symptomatic gallstones.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound, assuming a fetal or leaning-forward position for comfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration secondary to intractable vomiting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient in visible distress, diaphoretic, and adopting a leaning-forward posture.
Physical Exam: Abdomen exhibits marked epigastric tenderness with voluntary guarding but lacks rigid, board-like peritonism. Abdominal distension is noted with diminished bowel sounds indicating a secondary paralytic ileus. Mild scleral icterus is present, highly suggestive of a distally impacted common bile duct gallstone precipitating the attack. Cullen's and Grey Turner's signs are negative at this early stage. The presentation explicitly aligns with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, pain relief leaning forward, and profuse vomiting; serum lipase >3x normal is expected on urgent laboratory testing.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin at bedside containing bilious fluid.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Pancreatitis (Suspected Gallstone Aetiology) - Status: Exacerbated (Severe acute inflammatory episode)
Differentials:

Peptic Ulcer Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a rigid, board-like abdomen and the specific postural relief gained by sitting up and leaning forward.

Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain characteristics, postural relief, the presence of localized epigastric peritonism, and profuse bilious vomiting out of proportion to typical cardiac presentations.

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain predominantly radiating straight through to the back rather than specifically to the right scapula, and the presence of diminished bowel sounds indicating significant retroperitoneal inflammation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe episode of epigastric pain radiating to the back, accompanied by profuse vomiting and tachycardia. Objective findings of focal epigastric tenderness, mild jaundice, and diminished bowel sounds signify a high-risk presentation of acute gallstone pancreatitis. Urgent, after-hours medical evaluation was absolutely indicated to distinguish between a perforated viscus and acute pancreatitis, provide parenteral antiemetics, and determine the necessity for acute hospital admission. Deferring care until normal operating hours would have unequivocally resulted in hypovolaemia, severe electrolyte derangement, and potential pancreatic necrosis without timely intravenous fluid resuscitation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg and IM Ondansetron 4mg. Facilitated immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for massive IV fluid resuscitation, comprehensive laboratory assays (lipase), and urgent imaging.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the pain spikes, sit entirely upright and lean your chest forward toward your knees. This specific physical posture mechanically pulls the stomach and surrounding organs away from the severely inflamed pancreas sitting deep against your spine, instantly reducing the compressive pressure and providing a degree of natural pain relief while awaiting transport.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to ED is required. If the patient loses consciousness, if vomiting contains fresh blood, or if extreme lightheadedness occurs upon standing, call 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a presumptive diagnosis based on clinical signs; definitive diagnosis requires blood tests (lipase) and CT imaging at the hospital.
Clinical Course: Symptoms will rapidly escalate without massive intravenous fluid replacement and complete bowel rest. Hospitalization is universally required to prevent severe complications.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Proceed directly to the Emergency Department. If you experience sudden, worsening shortness of breath or altered mental state, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A gallstone has likely dropped down and temporarily blocked the exit tube of your pancreas. This has caused the powerful digestive juices inside the pancreas to back up and begin digesting the organ itself, causing massive inflammation and agonizing pain. The injections provided are temporary measures to reduce vomiting during your transfer to the hospital. The hospital must immediately start massive amounts of intravenous fluids to flush the pancreas and rest the bowel completely.
Actionable Steps: Do not eat or drink a single drop of water. Any fluid or food entering your stomach will trigger the pancreas to violently attempt to produce more enzymes, directly worsening the auto-digestion and the excruciating pain.

